{
  "id": "Compound::DB00816 - Disease::DOID:2841",
  "verbalization": "Orciprenaline, as an adrenergic beta2-agonist, is linked to Asthma through its inclusion in a broader class of compounds that act as bronchodilators. This relationship is supported by multiple paths, all of which connect Orciprenaline to Asthma via its inclusion in adrenergic beta2-agonists, which in turn treat Asthma. The shared lowest common ancestors—Orciprenaline and its counterparts like Pirbuterol, Indacaterol, and Salmeterol—highlight a common pharmacological mechanism involving the activation of beta2-receptors, which is a well-established pathway in asthma management. The evidence suggests that Orciprenaline, as part of this class, may exert its therapeutic effects through similar mechanisms, potentially offering a repurposed use for treating asthma. The integration of these paths underscores the role of adrenergic agonists in bronchodilation and their relevance to asthma, reinforcing the likelihood of Orciprenaline being considered for repositioning in asthma treatment.",
